WEBVTT

00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:02.880
Which of our logins
still end in a password?

00:00:03.325 --> 00:00:06.535
There are eight ways to prove who you are,
and each can fail.

00:00:07.883 --> 00:00:08.993
First, tokens.

00:00:09.413 --> 00:00:12.763
OAuth2 lets an app act for
you without your password.

00:00:13.208 --> 00:00:14.478
The risk is consent.

00:00:15.303 --> 00:00:18.613
A malicious app asks for broad scopes,
and someone clicks Allow.

00:00:18.963 --> 00:00:21.573
Access tokens work for whoever holds them,

00:00:21.843 --> 00:00:24.773
and a stolen refresh token
keeps working for weeks.

00:00:25.883 --> 00:00:27.433
Second, the legacy estate.

00:00:27.893 --> 00:00:31.643
Kerberos and NTLM keep
Windows domains running.

00:00:31.938 --> 00:00:36.048
Attackers crack service account
passwords offline, or replay hashes.

00:00:36.503 --> 00:00:40.593
LDAP and RADIUS sit behind VPNs and Wi-Fi,

00:00:40.823 --> 00:00:43.233
and a password is often all it takes.

00:00:43.468 --> 00:00:49.108
Microsoft plans to switch network
NTLM off by default in the next

00:00:49.108 --> 00:00:50.918
major Windows Server release.

00:00:52.017 --> 00:00:53.367
Third, static keys.

00:00:53.737 --> 00:00:58.947
A key that a script or tool sends with
every request is a password with no owner.

00:00:59.207 --> 00:01:02.737
It leaks into code,
tickets and chat, and rarely expires.

00:01:03.257 --> 00:01:06.437
Whoever finds one can do everything
it allows, from anywhere,

00:01:06.437 --> 00:01:08.647
until someone rotates it.

00:01:09.583 --> 00:01:11.173
Fourth, AI agents.

00:01:11.503 --> 00:01:15.993
They log in, hold credentials and act,
but they are not a person,

00:01:16.363 --> 00:01:18.813
and they do not do
multi-factor authentication.

00:01:19.418 --> 00:01:23.528
A line of text in a document
an agent reads can redirect it.

00:01:23.748 --> 00:01:28.558
It then uses its own valid token,
so every log entry looks legitimate.

00:01:29.717 --> 00:01:30.927
What good looks like.

00:01:31.212 --> 00:01:34.442
Passkeys for people,
built on the FIDO2 standard.

00:01:34.822 --> 00:01:36.712
The key is bound to the real domain,

00:01:37.122 --> 00:01:38.732
so a fake site gets nothing.

00:01:38.997 --> 00:01:42.047
Certificates with mutual TLS for services.

00:01:42.482 --> 00:01:43.272
One identity,

00:01:43.702 --> 00:01:46.292
one owner and a kill
switch for each agent.

00:01:46.572 --> 00:01:50.502
Passwords, SMS codes and
static keys do not hold up.

00:01:52.230 --> 00:01:53.440
A suggested first step.

00:01:53.725 --> 00:01:57.675
Start passkeys with administrators,
and anyone who can move money.

00:01:57.925 --> 00:02:03.755
Then check that lost-device recovery does
not fall back to SMS or a helpdesk call.

00:02:05.283 --> 00:02:09.213
Passwords, SMS codes and
static keys do not hold up.

00:02:09.418 --> 00:02:11.328
Passkeys and certificates do.

